{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-943131f0c4caaf57","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9017\n3\tepic_count: 2\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9017\n11\t\n12\t**Strategy**: Keyboard accessibility conformance for core Dashboard flows\n13\t\n14\t**Triage outcome**: Full decomposition (not below-threshold, not docs-only). Although the\n15\tstrategy is small, it spans two components (Dashboard, Documentation) and two distinct\n16\twork streams: frontend code remediation in the Dashboard, and substantive conformance\n17\tcontent authoring (VPAT update, accessibility statement rewrite and republication). The\n18\tmultiple-work-streams escape condition for below-threshold applies; a docs-only triage\n19\tdoes not apply because the Dashboard requires code changes.\n20\t\n21\t## Epic List\n22\t\n23\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n24\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Keyboard accessibility remediation for the four audited Dashboard flows | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Update and republish accessibility conformance documentation (VPAT and accessibility statement) | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P2 |\n27\t\n28\t## Dependency DAG\n29\t\n30\t```mermaid\n31\tgraph TD\n32\t    E001[\"E001: Keyboard accessibility remediation (Dashboard)\"] --> E002[\"E002: Conformance docs update and republish (Documentation)\"]\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\t## DAG Justification\n36\t\n37\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n38\t|------|------|-----------|\n39\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | The VPAT and accessibility statement must describe the fixed state, so the conformance documentation cannot be finalized until the keyboard accessibility fixes are implemented. This is the only Implementation epic in the strategy, so docs-authoring is blocked by it alone. |\n40\t\n41\tNo Investigation epics: the strategy's Open Questions section is empty and the audit\n42\treport enumerates the exact defects per flow, so there are no unknowns whose resolution\n43\twould change which epics exist or what they do.\n44\t\n45\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n46\t\n47\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n48\t|-----|----------|------|-------|\n49\t| HLR-1 | P1 | E001 | Four audited flows fully keyboard-operable; core remediation epic. |\n50\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E001 (bundled) — focus-indicator consistency applies to the same four UI surfaces and the same audit defect list as HLR-1; the indicator work is not separately schedulable from the operability fixes on those surfaces, so a split would touch every flow twice. | Same component, same team, one remediation pass. |\n51\t| HLR-3 | P2 | E002 | VPAT and accessibility statement update, republication, technical review. |\n52\t\n53\tAll HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n54\t\n55\t## Health Warnings\n56\t\n57\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted Teams table,\n58\t  no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional\n59\t  Requirements section. Decomposition proceeded from the HLRs, Affected Components list,\n60\t  and problem statement. Team assignments were inferred: the Dashboard team\n61\t  (RHOAI Dashboard) is corroborated by the architecture context file\n62\t  (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`); the\n63\t  Documentation owner (RHOAI Documentation) is inferred — no architecture-context file\n64\t  exists for the Documentation component to corroborate it.\n65\t- **Epic size above the ~2-week guideline (E001)**: E001 remediates four core flows\n66\t  (project creation, workbench spawn, model deployment, pipeline run submission); by\n67\t  typical modal/focus remediation effort this is plausibly ~2 sprints, above the\n68\t  2-week guideline. Kept whole because HLR-2's focus-indicator consistency spans all\n69\t  four flows as one shared pattern and the audit verification is a single pass — a\n70\t  per-flow split would fragment the shared indicator work and audit each flow twice.\n71\t  Re-split trigger: if remediation of the first two flows exceeds one sprint (pace\n72\t  projecting four flows past 2 sprints), split the remaining flows into per-flow epics\n73\t  under the same team, carrying the shared indicator-consistency work in the flow where\n74\t  the canonical pattern is established.\n75\t- **Possible priority understatement (non-blocking)**: HLR-3 is P2 (consistent with the\n76\t  strategy frontmatter priority `Normal` and the docs-authoring priority exception),\n77\t  but the problem statement ties the published VPAT to two public-sector customer\n78\t  renewals. If those renewals are near-term, the documentation epic is\n79\t  business-critical despite its P2 label — planners should confirm the renewal dates.\n80\t- **Scope trap avoided**: the strategy's Out of Scope excludes screen-reader semantics\n81\t  beyond what the keyboard fixes require; the conformance documentation epic must not\n82\t  claim broader conformance than the keyboard fixes deliver, or the VPAT would assert\n83\t  scope the implementation does not cover.\n84\t\n85\t## Tiered Delivery\n86\t\n87\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing. All work is internal to\n88\tthe Dashboard and Documentation components, and there are no upstream or third-party\n89\tdependencies.\n90\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83c55d8483d1c683","agent_id":"a029a4ff05a08bbb9","session_id":"85349235-cd13-47e8-af33-caa47bbf874d","uuid":"4fde5f3f-d047-44a3-94f2-739874cedb06","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:18:07.342Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-943131f0c4caaf57","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"